    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1799–1800</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s Combination Acts outlaw workers’ combinations; their repeal in 1824 lets trade unions organise openly</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1834</span><span class="rf-text">Six farm labourers from Tolpuddle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a> are sentenced to transportation for swearing a union oath</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1838</span><span class="rf-text">The People’s Charter launches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartism">Chartism</a>, a mass campaign for votes for working men</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1864</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">International Workingmen’s Association</a>, the First International, is founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1889</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>’s dockers win their strike; the Second International calls for May Day demonstrations for the eight-hour day</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text">The International Labour Organization is founded under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1926</span><span class="rf-text">The Trades Union Congress calls a nine-day General Strike in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> miners</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1980</span><span class="rf-text">Strikes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>’s shipyard lead to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a>, the first independent trade union recognised in the Soviet bloc</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/peterloo-massacre"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peterloo-massacre.jpg" alt="Peterloo Massacre" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/peterloo-massacre">Peterloo Massacre</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peterloo_Massacre.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Carlile, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Working people combined to defend their earnings long before the factory age. Journeymen in the crafts of early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> formed brotherhoods, such as the compagnonnages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and in 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> trade clubs and friendly societies collected weekly pennies to pay sickness and burial benefits and, when need arose, to support a strike. Where no organisation existed, protest took the form of bread riots, petitions and what the historian Eric Hobsbawm called “collective bargaining by riot”.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>, which began in Britain in the late 18th century, transformed work. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton</a> mills, ironworks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines gathered great numbers of wage-earners in towns such as Manchester and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, where men, women and children worked days of twelve hours or more under factory discipline, with no protection against injury, sickness or unemployment. Skilled handloom weavers and framework knitters saw their earnings collapse as machines and cheaper labour undercut them. Between 1811 and 1816 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luddites">Luddites</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> smashed machinery, and in 1812 Parliament made the breaking of stocking frames punishable by death. Demands for political reform met force as well: in 1819 yeomanry cavalry charged a peaceful crowd of tens of thousands at St Peter’s Field in Manchester, and an estimated eighteen people died in the massacre remembered as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterloo-massacre">Peterloo</a>.</p><p>The state and the law stood against combination. In France the Le Chapelier Law of 1791 banned associations of workers and employers alike, and Britain’s Combination Acts of 1799 and 1800, passed in wartime fear of revolution, made it a crime for workmen to combine to raise wages or shorten hours. Working men also lacked the vote: Britain’s Reform Act of 1832 enfranchised much of the middle class but left most of them out. The labour movement grew from the attempt to answer these problems together: low wages and long hours through trade unions, insecurity through mutual aid and cooperation, and exclusion from power through campaigns for the vote and, later, through parties of its own.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-marx.jpg" alt="Karl Marx" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Marx_001_restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Jabez Edwin Mayall, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first aims were practical: higher wages, shorter hours, safer workplaces and an end to child labour, won by collective bargaining between unions and employers and backed by the threat of a strike. Shorter hours were the most persistent demand. Short-time committees in the textile districts of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> campaigned for a ten-hour day, and the eight-hour day became the movement’s international goal; the slogan “eight hours’ labour, eight hours’ recreation, eight hours’ rest” is usually credited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-owen">Robert Owen</a>. Unions also sought recognition from employers and from the law: the right to organise, to strike and to picket without prosecution for conspiracy.</p><p>Self-help ran alongside bargaining. Unions and friendly societies paid sickness, unemployment and funeral benefits, and the cooperative movement, whose model was the shop opened in 1844 by the Rochdale Society of Equitable Pioneers, returned its profits to members as a dividend on their purchases. Workers founded reading rooms, schools and newspapers. Many also sought political power: first the vote for working men, the leading demand of the People’s Charter of 1838, which also called for the secret ballot, annual parliaments, equal constituencies, the payment of members of parliament and an end to the property qualification for them; then labour representatives in parliament and laws on hours, safety, insurance and pensions.</p><p>Beyond these shared demands the movement disagreed about its final goal. Owenite cooperators hoped to replace competition with cooperative communities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> and Friedrich Engels, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">Communist Manifesto</a> appeared in 1848, argued that the working class must take power and end the private ownership of the means of production. Anarchists and revolutionary syndicalists, strong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, trusted in direct action and the general strike rather than in parliaments, while reformist socialists such as the Fabians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> sought gradual change through elections and legislation. Christian trade unions took their lead from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a>’s encyclical Rerum novarum (1891), which defended workers’ right to form associations and to receive a just wage, and the American Federation of Labor kept largely to bargaining over pay and hours.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chartism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chartism.jpg" alt="Chartism" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chartism">Chartism</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chartist_meeting_on_Kennington_Common_by_William_Edward_Kilburn_1848_-_restoration1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William Edward Kilburn, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the Combination Acts were repealed in 1824, after lobbying by the radical tailor Francis Place, though a law of 1825 again restricted what unions could do. In 1834 six farm labourers from Tolpuddle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, led by the Methodist preacher George Loveless, were sentenced to transportation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> for swearing a secret oath; a petition of some 800,000 signatures and mass demonstrations won their pardon in 1836. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartism">Chartists</a> presented three great petitions to Parliament, in 1839, 1842 and 1848, the second with over three million signatures; all were rejected, and a rising at Newport in 1839 and a wave of strikes in 1842 were put down.</p><p>The movement became international in the second half of the century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">International Workingmen’s Association</a>, the First International, was founded at St Martin’s Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 28 September 1864; divided between the followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and the anarchists around Mikhail Bakunin, it split in 1872 and was dissolved in 1876. National federations followed, among them Britain’s Trades Union Congress in 1868 and the American Federation of Labor in 1886. In 1888 and 1889 strikes by London’s match workers and dockers brought unskilled labourers into “new unions”. The Second International, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in July 1889, called for demonstrations for the eight-hour day on 1 May 1890, a date proposed by the American Federation of Labor: American unions had struck for it on 1 May 1886, days before the Haymarket affair in Chicago. May Day became the workers’ day across the world.</p><p>In August 1914 most socialist parties supported their nations’ war efforts, and the Second International broke apart. In 1919 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> created the International Labour Organization, whose first convention set an eight-hour day and a 48-hour week in industry. In May 1926 Britain’s Trades Union Congress called a General Strike in support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> miners; about 1.7 million workers came out for nine days before it was called off. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in June 1936 a wave of factory occupations brought the Matignon Agreements and laws granting a 40-hour week and two weeks’ paid holiday. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> and Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> destroyed the independent unions. In August 1980 a strike at the Lenin Shipyard in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> won the right to form independent unions; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a>, founded that September as the first independent union recognised in the Soviet bloc, soon had about ten million members. Driven underground by martial law in December 1981, it was legalised again in April 1989 and swept the partly free elections that June.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/keir-hardie"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/keir-hardie.jpg" alt="Keir Hardie" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/keir-hardie">Keir Hardie</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Keir_Hardie_by_George_Grantham_Bain.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Grantham Bain / Adam Cuerden, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-owen">Robert Owen</a>, a Welsh-born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> manufacturer, shortened hours and opened schools at his mills at New Lanark in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, then turned to cooperative communities and, in 1834, to the short-lived Grand National Consolidated Trades Union. William Lovett, a cabinetmaker, helped draft the People’s Charter, and Feargus O’Connor, an Irish orator who owned the Northern Star newspaper, became the most popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartism">Chartist</a> leader. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flora-tristan">Flora Tristan</a> called in The Workers’ Union (1843) for an international union of all workers, men and women, five years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto">Communist Manifesto</a>; she died on a speaking tour in 1844. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and Engels gave much of the movement its theory: Marx wrote the founding address of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">First International</a>, and Engels’s The Condition of the Working Class in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> (1845) described the slums of Manchester.</p><p>The great organisers of the next generations included Ferdinand Lassalle and August Bebel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keir-hardie">Keir Hardie</a>, a Scottish miner who in 1892 became one of the first independent labour members of the British Parliament and helped found the Labour Party; Ben Tillett, Tom Mann and John Burns, leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> dock strike; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-gompers">Samuel Gompers</a>, a London-born cigar maker who led the American Federation of Labor almost without a break from 1886 to 1924; and Mary Macarthur, who organised women workers and in 1910 led the women chain makers of Cradley Heath to victory in their fight for a minimum wage. Socialists such as Jean Jaurès in France and Rosa Luxemburg in Germany debated reform, revolution and how to prevent a European war; Jaurès was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 31 July 1914, days before France went to war. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1980 the crane operator Anna Walentynowicz, whose dismissal set off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> strike, and the electrician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-walesa">Lech Wałęsa</a> stood at the head of the movement that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a>.</p><p>The movement’s strength, however, lay in its millions of ordinary members: miners, dockers, railway workers, textile hands and engineers who paid weekly dues, elected shop stewards and branch secretaries, marched behind painted banners on May Day and risked their jobs, and sometimes their lives, in strikes. Women workers, from the London match girls of 1888 to the garment workers of many countries, often had to organise against employers and against the suspicion of male trade unionists at the same time.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russian-revolution.jpg" alt="Russian Revolution" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9C%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B3_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%9D%D0%B5%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BC_%D0%BF%D1%80%D0%BE%D1%81%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B5_(1917).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unknown; photo retake by George Shuklin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The movement achieved much of its original programme. Trade unions were made lawful in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> by the Trade Union Act of 1871 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by a law of 1884, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the National Labor Relations Act of 1935 protected the right to organise and bargain collectively. Hours fell: Britain’s Factories Act of 1847 limited women and young people in textile mills to ten hours a day, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the eight-hour day spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In time Britain adopted all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartism">Chartists</a>’ points except annual parliaments, though constituencies were never made exactly equal: the property qualification for members of parliament went in 1858, the secret ballot came in 1872, their payment in 1911 and votes for all men in 1918. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> introduced insurance against sickness in 1883, accidents in 1884 and old age in 1889, partly to win workers away from socialism, and other countries followed. Labour parties came to power: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> a Labor government held national office briefly in 1904, the first in the world, and in Britain the Labour Party first formed a government in 1924. After 1945 welfare states and settled bargaining between unions and employers gave organised labour a recognised place in many societies.</p><p>There were also defeats and divisions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Commune of 1871 was crushed with thousands of deaths. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution of 1917</a> divided the movement between communists and social democrats for most of the century, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and its allies the unions became instruments of the ruling party, the model that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> defied. Many unions long excluded or neglected women, immigrants and Black workers, and parts of the movement in Australia and the United States campaigned to keep out Asian immigrants.</p><p>From the 1980s union membership fell in most industrial countries as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> and manufacturing declined and work moved into services. In Britain it fell from a peak of about 13 million in 1979, and the miners’ strike of 1984–1985 ended in defeat. Elsewhere unions gained new weight: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> the Congress of South African Trade Unions, founded in 1985, became a leading force against apartheid.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union.jpg" alt="Solidarity (Polish trade union)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity (Polish trade union)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strajk_sierpniowy_w_Stoczni_Gda%C5%84skiej_im._Lenina_34.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zenon Mirota, CC BY-SA 3.0 pl</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Much of what wage-earners in industrial countries take for granted was first a demand of the labour movement: the eight-hour day and the weekend, paid holidays, factory inspection, sick pay, pensions and the right to bargain collectively. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights">Universal Declaration of Human Rights</a> of 1948 counted the right to form and to join trade unions among human rights. The International Labour Organization, awarded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1969, sets international standards, among them conventions on freedom of association and the abolition of forced labour, and 1 May is a public holiday in many countries. The movement also left a culture of its own: banners, brass bands, choirs and sports clubs, cooperative shops, workers’ colleges and newspapers, and songs such as “The Internationale”, written by Eugène Pottier in 1871 and set to music by Pierre De Geyter in 1888.</p><p>Its history was first written by participants and sympathisers. Sidney and Beatrice Webb’s The History of Trade Unionism (1894) told it as the story of institutions. E. P. Thompson’s The Making of the English Working Class (1963) set out to rescue ordinary workers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luddites">Luddites</a> among them, from “the enormous condescension of posterity” and inspired a generation of history from below. Eric Hobsbawm explained the moderation of many British unions by the rise of a “labour aristocracy” of skilled workers, an idea taken from Engels and much debated since.</p><p>Later historians widened the view to women’s work and the household, to race and empire, and to the labour movements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, which older accounts had treated as late copies of a European model; global labour historians now study enslaved, indentured and informal workers alongside factory hands. Historians continue to debate why the movement took such different forms, revolutionary in some countries and reformist in others, and why socialism never became a mass force in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, a question the German economist Werner Sombart posed as early as 1906.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The labour movement, born with industry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> around 1800, organised wage-earners in trade unions, cooperatives and parties to win better pay, shorter hours, security and political rights. Persecuted at first, it spread across the world, won the eight-hour day, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-insurance">social insurance</a> and a place in government, and left a lasting mark on law, politics and everyday working life.</em></p>

